-- §20 What a WIDER elimination would do to the figure it comes off, and whether the filer licensed it.
# One row per filed elimination, with the reading it takes, the direction its own width pushes the
# composed figure, and what its claim says its bound rests on.
#
# The one place less knowledge gives a tighter answer
#   Every other width in this model widens what it touches. An elimination subtracted bound by bound
#   does the opposite: widen it and the composed figure narrows, because its low rises and its high
#   falls together. So a composer less certain how much was double counted files a more precise
#   composed demand. Nothing else here can see that, which is why it is worth a section.
#
# The licence is a filed answer, not a verdict
#   Reading an elimination bound by bound assumes its bounds move with the figure it comes out of, and
#   a filing can say exactly that at its claim's `boundOrigin`: a derivation naming `sharedParts` means
#   the figure is a copy of one stated in the part it removes. An absent origin says nothing sets the
#   bound, which neither supports the assumption nor contradicts it. So this column reports which of
#   the two the filer wrote down and judges neither.
#
# Reported, not asserted
#   Neither reading is a defect. The filings that come out anti-isotone and unlicensed are correct
#   under the reading they state, so a rule here would spend trust on documents that are right.
#
# ⛔ The figures are how the key is checked, and a count could not do it
#   `claim_seq` is an ordinal, so joining on it returns one claim per elimination whether or not it is
#   the right claim. Measured on this corpus: shifted by one it still returns every row and the figures
#   stop agreeing. So this section carries the claim's own three points beside the elimination's and
#   the program requires them equal, which is the check a cardinality could never be.
#
# Both arms are occupied, which is what makes the columns readable
#   A classification with an empty arm reports the corpus rather than the model. The crossed reading is
#   reached only where bound by bound would leave the claim disordered, so it is rare by construction
#   and not by accident, and a run in which it vanished would mean the fixture exercising it had
#   stopped.
-- eliminations/monotone.sqlc, ordered so the anti-isotone rows read first.
SELECT m.composition                     AS "composition!",
       m.composed_layer                  AS "composed_layer!",
       m.quantity::text                  AS "quantity!",
       m.low::float8                     AS low,
       m.high::float8                    AS high,
       m.width::float8                   AS width,
       m.crossed                         AS "crossed!",
       m.isotone                         AS isotone,
       m.licensed                        AS licensed,
       m.claim_seq                       AS claim_seq,
       m.claim_low::float8               AS claim_low,
       m.claim_mode::float8              AS claim_mode,
       m.claim_high::float8              AS claim_high,
       m.what_a_wider_elimination_does   AS "what_a_wider_elimination_does!",
       m.what_the_bound_rests_on         AS "what_the_bound_rests_on!"
FROM (
    :compose(eliminations/monotone.sqlc)
) m
ORDER BY (m.isotone IS NULL), m.isotone, m.licensed, 1, 2, 3
